  "summary": "Deep learning models for transcriptomics, proteins and pathology are now being tested against curated annotations and perturbation data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is a move from reporting predictive accuracy toward asking what learned representations actually encode. In transcriptomic and protein settings, interpretability claims are checked against external reference points: sparse autoencoder features on transcriptomic foundation models largely act as gene detectors on spatial panels, and conclusions from interpretability methods in protein language models (ESMC) depend on residue-level curated annotations. Other items build structure into the model instead of inspecting it after training. CIRCE applies a Boltzmann distribution over Hamming distance in combinatorial perturbation space to infer both input identity and graded signaling strength. CMC-VAE links genes and proteins causally across CITE-seq data and reports improved perturbation predictions after interventions. SimpleP2G addresses a different question, comparing image encoders and decoder designs for predicting gene expression from pathology images, with Virchow2 features and a simple frozen-embedding decoder highlighted.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
